From 812cdfee4d01c48784a0e4553e5d058807399f64 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: KnutMann <knutmann@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 02:37:47 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 15/17] Fetch the history the phone still remembers

WhatsApp keeps no readable history on its servers; the primary device
does. whatsmeow can ask it for the messages preceding a known one, and
the answer arrives as a HistorySync of type ON_DEMAND carrying whole
WebMessageInfo records: the message, the reactions it gathered, and how
far it travelled. The plug-in threw all of that away, with the
maintainer's own TODO marking the spot.

A conversation coming into being now asks the phone for what came
before, behind the new fetch-history-on-open account option (off by
default). The message cache supplies the anchor the request needs, and
holds the recent messages the phone will not repeat, so the two are
merged and sorted before display. Fetched lines travel the ordinary
display path, marked delayed, so they carry their message id like any
other message and a front-end can tell old news from new. Afterwards
the state the phone remembers is reported through the receipt and
reaction signals: a message reopened in a window can show its ticks
and its reaction chips again, the way it looks on the phone.

The phone answers only while WhatsApp runs there, and answers
asynchronously or not at all, so this stays opt-in and silent when it
fails.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
